<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1868 CE – 1918 CE · capital Zagreb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Croatian kingdom within the Hungarian half of Austria-Hungary, where modern Croatian institutions and the idea of South Slav unity took shape.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-hungarian-settlement">Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</a> of 1868, known as the Nagodba, joined the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> into one autonomous unit within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-crown-of-saint-stephen">Lands of the Hungarian Crown</a>. Croatia kept its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a> and control of internal affairs, justice and education, while finance and defence were common with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. The former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a> was added in 1881.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A ban appointed on the Hungarian prime minister's advice headed the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>. Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazuranic">Ivan Mažuranić</a> modernised schools and courts in the 1870s, while Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karoly-khuen-hedervary">Károly Khuen-Héderváry</a>, from 1883 to 1903, ruled by playing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-juraj-strossmayer">Strossmayer</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-academy-of-sciences-and-arts">Yugoslav Academy</a> and helped open the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-zagreb">University of Zagreb</a> in 1874. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-fiume">Fiume</a> remained under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1905 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croat-serb-coalition">Croat-Serb Coalition</a> dominated politics. On 29 October 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a> broke all ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a>, which in December merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: DIREKTOR (parts derived from User:Ex13's images) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Croatia-Slavonia_with_CoA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1868 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zagreb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ivan Mažuranić, Károly Khuen-Héderváry</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Croatia, Serbia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Croatia-Slavonia, Kraljevina Hrvatska i Slavonija</dd></dl></aside></div>
